From 3aa41cbc74dc31233aed0e424f70d6cb3591335f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Loitsch <florian@loitsch.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:08:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [Xtensa] Keep the stack pointer 16-byte aligned at calls

The Xtensa ABIs keep SP 16-byte aligned (GCC: STACK_BOUNDARY 128), and the
prologue rounds every frame to 16 bytes, but XtensaFrameLowering declared
a stack alignment of 4. When the call frame is not reserved (the function
has a frame pointer or a variable-sized object), a call with stack
arguments moved SP by their size rounded to 4 only, so the callee ran with
SP at 4, 8 or 12 mod 16. A callee that computes the address of a field of
an 8- or 16-byte aligned local with `or` (from the known alignment) then
got a wrong address, with no fault.

- Declare a stack alignment of 16 (and a transient alignment of 16).
- LowerCall: CALLSEQ_END releases the rounded size that CALLSEQ_START
  reserved (it used the unrounded size, which only matched while the
  alignment was 4; the machine verifier catches the mismatch).

Variable-sized objects are now rounded to 16 bytes, and call0 frames are
multiples of 16 bytes as with GCC; the call0 tests are updated for the
new frame offsets.

Fixes espressif/llvm-project#142 (esp-rs/rust#286).
